Distributed Web Infrastructure - www.foobar.com
=================================================

Infrastructure overview
------------------------

  [User browser]
       |
  www.foobar.com → DNS → load balancer IP
       |
  [HAproxy - Load Balancer]
       |              |
  [Server 1]      [Server 2]
  ├── Nginx        ├── Nginx
  ├── App server   ├── App server
  └── App files    └── App files
       |                  |
       └──────┬───────────┘
          [MySQL cluster]
          Primary (Server 1) ←→ Replica (Server 2)


Why each element was added
---------------------------

2 servers instead of 1
  To eliminate the single point of failure from the previous design. If one server
  goes down, the other keeps the site up. It also lets us split the load.

HAproxy (load balancer)
  It sits in front of both servers and distributes incoming requests between them.
  Without it, users would all hit one server and we'd be back to the same bottleneck.

MySQL Primary-Replica cluster
  One database alone is a SPOF and can't handle high read traffic well. A replica
  offloads reads and acts as a backup if the primary fails.


Q&A
----

What distribution algorithm is HAproxy using?
  Round Robin — it sends each new request to the next server in line, cycling through
  all available servers equally. Simple and works well when servers have similar specs.

Active-Active vs Active-Passive — which one is this, and what's the difference?

  This setup is Active-Active: both servers are running and handling traffic at the
  same time. HAproxy splits requests between them.

  Active-Passive means only one server handles traffic at a time. The second one sits
  idle and only kicks in if the first one fails (failover). No load sharing happens.

How does a Primary-Replica (Master-Slave) database cluster work?
  The Primary node handles all write operations (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE).
  Every write is then replicated — copied — to the Replica node automatically.
  The Replica stays in sync and handles read queries, taking load off the Primary.

Primary vs Replica from the application's perspective
  The app sends writes (create, update, delete) to the Primary only.
  The app can send reads (SELECT) to the Replica to reduce load on the Primary.
  If the app doesn't distinguish between the two, everything hits the Primary anyway.


Issues with this infrastructure
---------------------------------

SPOFs
  - The load balancer is a single point of failure. If HAproxy goes down, no traffic
    reaches either server.
  - The Primary database is also a SPOF for writes. If it fails, the site can still
    serve reads from the Replica, but nothing can be written until failover happens.

No firewall
  The servers are exposed directly to the internet with no filtering. Anyone can
  attempt connections on any port, making the infrastructure vulnerable to attacks.

No HTTPS
  Traffic between users and the load balancer is unencrypted. Passwords, session
  tokens, and sensitive data are sent in plain text and can be intercepted.

No monitoring
  There's no visibility into server health, traffic, errors, or resource usage.
  If a server goes down or starts degrading, nobody gets alerted — you find out when
  users complain.


